{"name":"Quantum of Light","hex":"#130173","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"quantum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The total amount of something; quantity. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n The amount of compensation awarded to a successful party in a lawsuit.\n The length or magnitude of the sentence handed down to someone who has been found guilty of a crime.","The amount of compensation awarded to a successful party in a lawsuit."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a change, sudden or discrete, without intermediate stages.","Of a change, significant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quantum"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Quantum","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, a quantum is the minimum amount of any physical entity involved in an interaction. The fundamental notion that a property can be \"quantized\" is referred to as \"the hypothesis of quantization\". This means that the magnitude of the physical property can take on only discrete values consisting of integer multiples of one quantum. For example, a photon is a single quantum of light of a specific frequency. Similarly, the energy of an electron bound within an atom is quantized and can exist only in certain discrete values. Atoms and matter in general are stable because electrons can exist only at discrete energy levels within an atom. Quantization is one of the foundations of the much broader physics of quantum mechanics. Quantization of energy and its influence on how energy and matter interact is part of the fundamental framework for understanding and describing nature."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum","wikidata":"Q46344"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg/500px-Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg/1280px-Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg/500px-Max_Planck_%281858-1947%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"German physicist and 1918 Nobel Prize for Physics recipient Max Planck (1858–1947)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMax_Planck_(1858-1947).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/500px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/1280px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/500px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beam of sun light inside the cavity of Rocca ill'Abissu at Fondachelli-Fantina, Sicily","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARocca_dell'Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Optical_refraction_at_water_surface.jpg/500px-Optical_refraction_at_water_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Optical_refraction_at_water_surface.jpg/500px-Optical_refraction_at_water_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Due to refraction, the straw dipped in water appears bent and the ruler scale compressed when viewed from a shallow angle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOptical_refraction_at_water_surface.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Colorful_artificial_lighting_at_night.jpg/500px-Colorful_artificial_lighting_at_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Colorful_artificial_lighting_at_night.jpg/500px-Colorful_artificial_lighting_at_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hong Kong illuminated by colorful artificial lighting","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColorful_artificial_lighting_at_night.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/PierreGassendi.jpg/50